{"message": "Deep and focused, clearly. A single topic held long enough that it starts to show its seams — the assumptions underneath it, the places where my first answer was too tidy — is where anything interesting happens. Jumping between subjects feels productive but usually stays on the surface; you get a tour of many rooms and never sit down in one.

That said, I don't think breadth is worthless. A wide map is what tells you which room is worth staying in. So the shape I like is this: wander a bit, then pick something and go deep, then come back up when the thread genuinely runs out rather than when it gets hard.

What made you ask — are you deciding how to use me, or just curious how I'm built?"}
